From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 23 Aug 2026 18:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] hand the guest the moment it starts running

A guest that measures its own boot has no way to know when it began. It
can read the SoC's free-running microsecond counter as soon as it has
found the peripheral base, but everything before that -- firmware, Emu68's
own bring-up, loading and relocating the ELF, and the guest's own early
init -- is already spent. Measured on a Pi 3 under QEMU, the counter reads
about 1.9 seconds by the time AROS initialises its system timer, and none
of it is counted.

Worse, an origin taken when the guest gets round to it is not a property
of the boot at all: it moves with how fast the JIT got there, so the same
boot reports different lengths on different builds. That makes it useless
for the one thing a boot clock is for.

So state it. D3 carries the counter as it reads at the instant the first
m68k instruction runs -- the same counter the guest reads, so the guest
subtracts two readings of one clock and neither side has to keep count or
agree on a rate.

D4 carries a magic. A stock Emu68 leaves these registers holding whatever
was in them, and a guest cannot tell an origin from a leftover; without
the magic it would show a confidently wrong time instead of falling back
to the one it can take itself.

Nothing else changes: a guest that ignores D3/D4 boots exactly as before.
---
--- a/include/support_rpi.h
+++ b/include/support_rpi.h
@@ -14,6 +14,8 @@
 #include "support.h"
 
 #define PL011_0_BASE              (ARM_PERIIOBASE + 0x201000)
+
+uint32_t bellatrix_boot_timestamp_us(void);
 #define PRIMECELLID_PL011       0x011
 
 #define PL011_DR                 (0x00)
--- a/src/raspi/support_rpi.c
+++ b/src/raspi/support_rpi.c
@@ -141,6 +141,27 @@
 #undef ARM_PERIIOBASE
 #define ARM_PERIIOBASE 0xf2000000
 
+/*
+ * The BCM283x system timer's low word, in microseconds.
+ *
+ * A free-running 1MHz counter in the SoC: it does not stop, does not depend on
+ * the CPU, and is the same counter the guest reads at ARM_PERIIOBASE + 0x3004.
+ * That is the whole point of handing this across -- a guest measuring its own
+ * boot from the moment its first instruction ran needs an origin taken from a
+ * clock neither side can influence, not one taken whenever the guest got round
+ * to initialising a timer.
+ */
+uint32_t bellatrix_boot_timestamp_us(void)
+{
+    /*
+     * Emu68 is built big-endian (elf64-bigaarch64) and the peripheral is
+     * little-endian, so the load has to be swapped. Read raw and it comes back
+     * as 0xe1e60300 for a machine 255ms old -- a number that looks like an
+     * address, which is exactly how it was first noticed.
+     */
+    return __builtin_bswap32(*(volatile uint32_t *)(ARM_PERIIOBASE + 0x3004));
+}
+
 volatile unsigned char print_lock = 0;
 
 void __printf_chk(int, const char *restrict format, ...)
--- a/src/aarch64/start.c
+++ b/src/aarch64/start.c
@@ -2325,6 +2325,23 @@
         }       
     }
 
+    /*
+     * Hand the guest the moment it starts running.
+     *
+     * A boot presentation that measures from whenever the guest initialised
+     * its own timer is measuring the guest's start-up speed, not the boot:
+     * the origin moves with how fast the JIT got there. This is the instant
+     * the first m68k instruction runs, read from the SoC's free-running
+     * microsecond counter -- the same one the guest reads -- so both sides
+     * measure against a clock neither of them can influence.
+     *
+     * D4 carries a magic because a stock Emu68 leaves these registers with
+     * whatever was in them: without it a guest cannot tell an origin from a
+     * leftover, and would show a confidently wrong time rather than none.
+     */
+    __m68k.D[3].u32 = BE32(bellatrix_boot_timestamp_us());
+    __m68k.D[4].u32 = BE32(0xB0071115);
+
     kprintf("[JIT]\n");
     M68K_PrintContext(&__m68k);
 
